   183	He looked round the shed. There was the drum, as tall as his chest and as thick as a cart, with the hauling rope coiled against its foot.
   184	
   185	He went from the door post to the drum's far side. The crack came, and the straw, and the lock; and he stood out one count behind four feet of oak, where nothing in that shed could have reached him. It was the same count that it had always been, and for once it had cost him nothing.
   186	
   187	"There," said Lira.
   188	
   189	It was full dark outside the hurdles by then, and the quietest of the ten fragments had come up with the dark, as it did at every dusk. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as far as his knees. It was the only fragment that charged him for being shut, a little, from the minute he woke, like a lamp left burning in a room that nobody used. It could have made less of him. He could have stood in that lantern's light and not been there at all.
   190	
   191	He held it where it was. It was a defector's craft, and in any hands but its owner's it would tell a trained eye exactly who had stood near enough to teach it; and the man who had stood near enough was ten feet off, with his back turned, keeping a door. Cael had opened it twice out here, where nobody could see who might carry the seeing west. He did not mean to open it a third time for the sake of a page in a book.
   192	
   193	"Again," said Lira. "Behind the drum. I want to see you like it."
   194	
   195	She had not got down from the block. He saw what it cost her to sit there and count another person's bursts with her own arm tied, and he knew better than to say so. He went back to the post.
   196	
   197	---
   198	
   199	Pike brought his book out to the plank at noon on the hundred and thirty-third day, for the first time since the ferry shut, and there were three slips on twelve feet of oak.
   200	
   201	Cael read them from the left-hand post. Each was on bark, in the square black hand, with a little picture beside the words for people who had no letters.
   202	
   203	*Roof, the ferryman's rope-house; shingles found; five, when it has kept a thaw out.*
   204	
   205	*The well-house at the south fork; a door hung and a hearthstone laid; three, and flour.*
   206	
   207	*Fence, the axe-head hold, the long one; forty. HOLD.* There were five short strokes at the foot of that one already.
   208	
   209	"That's the board?" said Lira.
   210	
   211	"That's a cold term." Pike set the horn pot down into its hole. "A roof, a well-house and a fence, and the fence is yours. The holds shut their gates and mend what they've got. I've had years with nothing on this oak from first snow to first boat but the nails."
   212	
   213	He opened the ledger at their heading and laid his stone on the leaf.
   214	
   215	"And there's the other matter, which I'd as soon do in daylight. You're stopping."
   216	
   217	"We're stopping," said Cael.
   218	
   219	The keeper had come out to his own door, behind the plank, with the cloth over his shoulder, since it was his floor that was to be priced.
   220	
   221	"Meadow rate is four a month for a crew that's on my sheet," said Pike.
   222	
   223	The keeper said, "Pike," and Pike said, "Eleventh."
   224	
   225	"What's it buy?" said Brom.
   226	
   227	"Standing." Pike considered him in his double fashion, and seemed to find the question a proper one. "That's the hearth's end you're lying at, which is the keeper's floor and my money, and I'll not explain that twice in one day. It's the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; he'll charge you for a second and he'll be right. And it's your names said to anybody who comes asking after a crew, which nobody will, the river being shut. That's three things."
   228	
   229	He held up three fingers of the only hand that he had, and looked at them, and looked at them again.
   230	
   231	"It's not bread. It's not candles. It's not the room behind his chimney; you've paid him that, six, and I have it down as heard and not as seen. And it's not that mule."
   232	
   233	"She's in the ferryman's shed," said Brom.
   234	
   235	"I know where she is. It's his shed, and his straw, and he's not asked you for it."
   236	
   237	"No."
   238	
   239	"He will." Pike dipped his pen. "Four a month, and a cold term's five by my book. Twenty, in front. I'll not chase a crew over ice for a month's standing. Four a month; five months; twenty, in front."
   240	
   241	Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
   242	
   243	"Seventy-six in hand," said Brom.
   244	
   245	"I had got as far as that," said Karis.
   246	
   247	Pike wrote it, and said it over to the meadow, as he said everything. Forty fires had burned along that water in the dry months, with a cart or a tent to each. There were three now, and a great many black rings in the snow where the others had been; the hide-woman sat at the nearest with a skin across her knees, and she was the only person in half a mile of grass who lifted her head to hear what a crew had paid. Then his pen went down the leaf to the last line under their names, which was the season's, and stopped beside the ruled space at the end of it.
   248	
   249	"You've not found your column," said Lira.
   250	
