   252	
   253	He shut the ledger on it.
   254	
   255	It was Brom who saw the sacks. There was a boy of sixteen or so by the smith's rail, on the far side of the inn yard, trying to get two of them onto his shoulder where he had been given one; they were the long grey kind that meant salt, and a dozen more lay on the snow beside a pair of mules. A lean old woman in a cap stood by with a staff that she did not lean on. A tall figure held the mules' heads, with a spear grounded, and a broad man was easing his weight onto his right leg.
