   282	
   283	He ruled a line under that, and began a new leaf. He headed it as he had never headed one before.
   284	
   285	*The ten, by use. Trials, in the ferryman's shed, at night, with the door kept. Not uses. Not counted.*
   286	
   287	*Wind, from Lira: three bursts across the horse-walk, and then more behind the drum. The landing locks for one count whatever the distance. It can't be shortened. It can be put somewhere. Her word for it: a way of leaving.*
   288	
   289	*Ember, from Karis: one point, on a wick. The heat comes back to the hand that carries the thing. The left palm.*
   290	
   291	*Shadow: came up at dusk as far as my knees. Held. Not opened, and it won't be.*
   292	
   293	*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
   294	
   295	He read the leaf over. It was a poor thing beside the inventory that he had made four nights ago, which had ten names in it, and a still place, and two promises. This had three fragments and the small plain facts of two evenings in a shed, and under them he had ruled seven lines and left them empty.
   296	
   297	He had made lists all his life of what he was and what he owed. He had never made one of what he did not know, with the gaps left standing in it on purpose; and he saw who had shown him that a book might be kept so. A one-armed man had turned a ledger round at first snow, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the place where nothing was written.
   298	
   299	On the far side of the lamp Karis had the hide flat under her four pebbles. She was not ruling anything. She had the older strip of brass laid along her pencil line, and she was sliding it up the skin a hand at a time, past the fourth ring and off the edge, onto the bare deal, as though the table might have something to say about where the line went next.
   300	
   301	There were seven more fragments to do. He looked at the room that he had left for them, and then at the figures above, and the thought came back to him that had come on the landing.
   302	
   303	"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."
   304	
   305	Karis did not look up. It was Lira who answered him, from the doorway, where she stood on the whole of her foot with a bowl in her right hand.
   306	
   307	"Then count something else," she said. "You've a leaf there with seven empty lines on it. I'd start with those."

