   163	"A hair," she said.
   164	
   165	"A hair," he said. "In my hands. Only mine."
   166	
   167	She thought about it. He watched her think about it and not theorize, which was the thing he had walked two days to watch, though he had not known that was what he was walking for. "I put my hands on you four times," she said, "and got nothing three times and a floor the fourth. Now a cord gets nothing. I don't know what that is." She took her hands off the table. "Neither do you. Write that down too."
   168	
   169	"I did."
   170	
   171	"Good," she said, and looked at the map once more, and then at Lira, who was standing by the door on two feet with her hands at her sides as if she did not know what to do with the second one, and then at him. "Lowmarch is the first stop on my spring. Millrace is the second. I'd have been at Pike's plank in six days with or without a map. I'll be there in two. Get the mule."
   172	
   173	Nobody had asked her. He noted that — he noted everything — and put it in the place where he kept the things that were true about her, and went to get the mule.
   174	
   175	---
   176	
   177	Six at Lowmarch on the two hundred and sixty-eighth day, with the ferryman's ropes rigged on the landing and the river running black and loud between banks that had stopped being white, and the meadow above the ford with the first three tents on it, and Pike's board with fourteen slips where there had been eleven, and the sixth of them standing at the plank with her roll on her back while Pike said, "That one," without looking up, and wrote a mark by a slip, and the room's five men who did not know her looked at the healer's roll and went back to their beer, because a healer was a healer and a plank was a plank.
   178	
   179	He catalogued the six of them at the inn's table that night, because it was the first table there had been six at since a marsh, and he wanted the count honest.
   180	
   181	Lira with two arms on the table and a foot under it that went down flat because she chose to, and Oryn's crossbow on the wall behind her where she had hung it in the autumn and had not been asked for it. Brom with the pouch — a hundred and eighteen in hand, the mule's shed paid, five, and the ferryman's rope-house roof done for seven in the last week of the ice — and his right shoulder carried as it had been carried since the binding, still and forward, which Oryn had looked at once across the inn's table that night and had said nothing about, which meant she would. Karis with the three copies in their oilcloth on the table's corner and the kit's ledger under her hand and the map under the ledger and both straightedges on the map, because Karis did not put a thing down that she was still using and she was still using all of it. Seln at the door end with the case against his leg and his eyes on the landing door, which had a spring's worth of strangers coming through it now, three tents' worth, and every one of them was a face he had not seen, which was the thing Seln did with a face. Oryn with her hands flat on the table and her roll on the floor by her chair and her route in her head, two stops of it here and one a day south, and the rest of it north, waiting.
   182	
   183	And himself. Ten. The still place at the middle of them that he did not touch. No tier, no rank, no file he could see, in a town where nobody had asked him for one in nine months, and where the not-asking had stopped, some time in the winter, being the thing it had been in the autumn. He could not have said when. A country that does not read you is a country that does not answer, either, and he had written that down in the hundred and sixties and had not liked it and had let it stand.
   184	
   185	Karis made her case then, because there were six, and she had been waiting for six.
   186	
   187	She made it plainly, in the order she had it, with her hand on the ledger. The carvings were instructions. She had known it at the Hall, from the shape of them — a record describes and an instruction commands, and these commanded, in the way a ledger's columns command — and she could not read a word of the language they commanded in, and neither could anyone on this side of the Line, because the language was older than the Line. The kit's ledger carried one entry for the Stair that was not a measurement — and under it a line that stopped, third site confirmed on bearing — a designation, in an index, in the surveyor's own hand, and she did not know what the index was, only that it was one, because a man does not write *index* in his own field book for his own convenience; he writes it because somebody will check it against a book he has never seen. The bearing ran east off the map and the answer to what it ran toward was not east.
   188	
   189	"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
   190	
   191	Nobody answered. Brom looked at the pouch. Seln looked at the door. Lira looked at Cael, because Lira always looked at Cael when the word *registry* was said in a room, and had done since Norhold, and did not need to say why. Oryn looked at the map upside down.
   192	
   193	"Inside walls," Oryn said.
   194	
   195	"Inside walls."
   196	
   197	"With a station in them."
   198	
   199	"At the top of the town. He doesn't go near it."
   200	
   201	Oryn said nothing to that, which was not agreement, and was not the other thing. It was the kind of nothing that a healer says when a patient she cannot read is about to walk toward the one place on the continent a healer's whole training says to keep an unreadable body away from, and has not asked her, and she has not decided what she would say if he did. Cael saw it. He did not answer it. It was hers, and she would say it when she said it, and the ferry had not run.
   202	
   203	Pike, at the plank across the room, wrote a line in his book and blotted it, and did not look up, and the room's noise went on round the six of them like a river round a stone.
   204	
   205	---
   206	
   207	*Two hundred and seventieth day. Lowmarch, the lower inn, by the fire. Ice going. Six of us.*
   208	
   209	*A hundred and eighteen in hand. Fence paid. Rope-house paid. Mule's shed paid. Nothing owed. Lira: two arms. A foot that's hers. Brom: the shoulder bound and carried and not yet read. Karis: three copies, one ledger, one map, two edges. Seln: the case. Oryn: her route, two stops of it here. Me: ten; the still place; a cord that reads everyone else.*
   210	
   211	*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. I've spent five years staying out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:Everything was on it. The Ost was there, and the river towns, and Auremont and Norhold and Greyvane had their names in small capitals. Every district had its bound in a fine line and every station a mark like a seed, and Denvash was at the far left edge, so small that he had to look twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:295:This one had the name on its cover that the instruments used. It was thick; it ran from a single leaf, taken down in a district office at the far end of the country, to four lines sent up that week from a seat at Ostrand. The clerk had those four lines on a slip and was to enter them at the file's foot, in order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:303:*Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:331:"The file," said Seln. "I have been thinking of it, since I shall not be let stand a watch. A subject who withdraws is closed. A subject who withdraws and then goes where no district runs is a different matter, and there is a line for that; I have copied it on to other people's files, in my time." He turned the cup. "It says *non-compliance*, which is an odd word for a man who has complied with everything. Then it says that the priority of observation is escalated. It is a form. It means that somebody is told to look harder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He did not put it in the book. It was only a column in his head, and each day it was shorter than the day before. On the first day it had run to a dozen things: metalled stone underfoot, ditches, dressed milestones with the district's device, a keeper at the ford with a stamp, a chartered sign, a board of hours. On the second day there had been a post bag on a hook, sealed in lead, and a notice of tolls in the registry's print, and a rider in district grey going west at a trot, who had not looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:*By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:"So it says." The keeper had hung his lantern on its nail. "I've never seen one come back in under thirty. It goes down that hill in a bag, when there's a bag, and somebody looks at it, and it comes up again. A man could walk to the district and back in the time, and some have." He lowered himself onto the bench by the fire. "Mostly they don't wait. They read that, and they stand where you're standing, and work it out, and then they go on east without, or they go home."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:21:Nobody answered, and no house stood by to produce anybody. The president let the full hour run by the district's clock, as the code required. She kept her feet for the whole of it, and opened her mouth only to ask the time. At the hour's end she directed the junior chair to enter what had occurred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:The house on the bluff answered first, in a registrar's upright hand. No enrollment stood, and no sponsorship; no floor hours were held, and no travel paper had been issued under the house's seal that had not lapsed with the form. The district seat answered that it kept nothing in that name but the file. The archive at Ternhall returned that the privileges in question had been surrendered at a counter, by their holder, before the instrument was dated. A provincial registry returned that the practitioner named as an associate stood at Copper, as he had for years; it added that a finding in his favour lay unsigned, and that the registry could not restrict a rank which nobody had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:He was not anywhere. A watcher's line had him on the east road, four days out, with a cart, and there was no line after that. Beyond the last station no district ran, and a restriction must run in a district; it wanted a counter to be served at, and a book to be written in. The code had foreseen that, as it foresaw most things. The clerk found the phrase in a schedule and wrote it at the instrument's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:*Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:53:He put it in a drawer. It would lie there, sealed and entire, for as long as it must, and on the day that any one of four names was written in a book inside a district, it would be taken out and served within the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:77:*Pending jurisdiction.* So they had gone where no district ran.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:131:The road ran out of the town between garden walls and then between hedges. It was a good road, metalled, and ditched on both sides, and at every thousand paces or so a dressed stone stood on the verge with a number cut in it and the district's device above the number, the river and the scale. He counted those, since he could not help it. *One*, with a blackbird on it. *Two.*

