   837	- **The road beyond the Hall (flag):** a made road an hour east of the marsh, with holds on it (two shut for the cold term, a third long dead, with one gable standing); the hide carters use it; it forks at a heap of chock-stones about a day on. The left hand climbs to a notch between two hills and is the gap to Thornwater, four days, coming down onto the Fallow road and in by Thornwater's eastern gate. The right hand goes on under a long bare ridge, "a great whale's back", a mile broad, which ends in a crest above the fourth site.
   838	- **The fourth site, as seen (flag): OUTSIDE ONLY.** From the crest at dusk on the 123rd: "a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills… and open toward the south"; no bird or beast; in the middle a thing "grey, and upright… taller than a man", with "a top that was not level" (Lira: "It's not one thing, I think. Or it is, and there's sky in it. I'd be guessing."). Two faint old ruts, with turf in them, go round the valley's head high up. **The cordon** lies about a hundred and ninety paces below those ruts: bars of the registry's pattern, one to a pace, each stamped *thirty-four* with the stroke and the bar beneath; Lira counts forty to the left and Brom forty to the right, and it goes on. **Lira stops one pace above the iron, at about a hundred and eighty-nine paces; her sigil is not tried; nobody says "Gone" or "Back"; nobody touches a stake; nobody crosses.** Cael "knew only that he had crossed three such lines before and that the fragments had answered there; nobody had tested this line, and nobody would." Seln puts no date on the cordon and nobody assigns a purpose to whoever set it. Karis lays the long cord twenty times along the feet of the bars, from outside: the short measure comes to half of what it has always come to. Her notebook's sum: **four hundred and forty yards, and two strides**. What the line does to a Path is unknown; Lira's question stands unanswered. Seln: "There was a sheet with two… A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
   839	- **Pike's first snow (flag; ruled KEEP):** "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw." He has done forty that day. The ninth line is given in narration (a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting; taken; six gone in; instruments found, brought up and kept by the crew that found them; six come out). The clerk "has been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to", and is given nothing. Pike turns the ledger toward Cael for the first time in this edition.
   840	- **The inn (flag):** the lower inn's keeper is "a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder". The back room is "the store behind the chimney": narrow, one small window, a deal table, meal-sacks, warm from the back of the hearth. Six marks for the cold term; the five may lie at the hearth's end "for the standing-money… as long as there's paper in my store". A small window at the far end of the hearth looks down on the landing. **On the table:** the kit's case in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid; inside it a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point (never driven), and a mallet; beside it the dead man's ledger, shut; the three boxes against the warm wall; fifty-two sheets in three heaps.
   841	- **The fence:** "the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate"; a day's posts still to set and the whole far side to mend before the thaw; "A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
   842	- **The last ferry (flag):** on the 130th, at noon, through a lane cut in the shelf ice; the post comes down to the far bank with the courier of day 4 (the hat with one side cut away). **Nine pieces:** the smith; the upper inn (a bill); two for the landing broker under his own seal; the hide-woman; a hold at the top of the north water; Teague's healer, from her daughter; Thornwater; and Hesk's parcel ("From a town I never heard of"). Hesk's straightedge is "a hand longer than the one from Ternhall, and a little broader". Cael's two letters go back by the same boat, with the clerk's packet. The ferryman and his boy take the hauling rope off the drum and coil it in the shed; the gelding goes to the smith's stable. The rope is still owed: "I'll not want it this side of the thaw."
   843	- **Oryn, days 122–128 (flag):** four days through the gap with the jenny; Thornwater at dusk on the 125th; the bowman's two fingers, for Lira. **The old woman of the hurdle winters at Thornwater, at her daughter's hearth; Oryn had told her at the mill, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee there after the thaw.** She rested it ten days, as bid, and then walked the day and a half over the low place in two, with a grandson to carry the bread; she is first on the bench on the 126th; Oryn reads the knee (mended at Millrace on the 113th) and it has held. Seven on the bench, two mendings. Word by the stooped carter on the 127th: the mill a crushed thumb; two gates no burials; at the ford a finger lost to a chain and the hand kept; the child's breathing no different. On the evening of the 128th the steading's woman gives her bark, ink and a quill; the carter goes down at first light on the 129th and will not come up again before the thaw. She means to go north by the wicket when the bench is paid.
   844	- **Roles (all unnamed):** Teague's healer; the hide carter (a scarf over his nose); the inn's keeper; the ferryman and his boy; the courier; the old woman and one grandson; the stooped carter; the Thornwater carter.
   845	
   846	### BOOK END STATE — for Book 8 (author, as updated after repair; the rulings above govern)
   847	**Calendar.** The morning of the **hundred and thirty-first day past the Line**, at the hearth of the lower inn at Lowmarch, with a hand's depth of snow on the sill. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term", "frost", "the thaw", "first snow", "a ring round the moon"). Days on the page: 115 (dusk), 116, 117, 118, 119, 120, 121, 122, 123, 124–128, 129, 130, 131; in the windows, 117–120 (Teague), 119–120 (Karis), 124 and 127 (Seln), 122–128 (Oryn). Weather: a mild spell on the 116th–118th; hard frost from the 120th; a ring round the moon on the 126th; a north wind from the 127th; the first flakes at Lowmarch on the afternoon of the 129th; two fingers of snow on the 130th and a hand on the 131st. Shelf ice a cart's length broad on each bank. **The ferry made its last run on the 130th and its hauling rope is taken in.**
   848	
   849	**Where everyone is.** Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln at the lower inn, sleeping at the hearth's end, with the store behind the chimney for the paper. Oryn at Thornwater, two days up a saddle that the carter does not expect to cross again before the thaw; she meant to go north by the wicket when the bench was paid, and her letter was written on the evening of the 128th. Teague, the spear, the bow, the boy and the older healer at Lowmarch for the cold term.
   850	
   851	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven** (the ford 1; the Ring 12; Oryn on the ridge 1; the Stair 9; the Hall 3; Oryn at the marsh 1). The trial point at the Hall's kerb on the 118th is logged and not counted. Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor: looked three times; unbound once (the Stair); a point set twice, of which one is the uncounted trial and one held the breach. Tide: Oryn twice, each time by her leave; himself three times (102, 119, 120); nobody else; never inside a line. **The still place:** his old log label, now a location under the breastbone, read three times; nothing moves in it; all ten go round it and none through; they do not pile against it; not the Quiet; not a fragment; told to Oryn at once and in order on the 120th, and to the other four in three sentences on the 121st; he has been told to write it down and stop touching it, and has not touched it since. Body: ribs quiet since the morning of the 129th; right forearm and left palm no longer remarked; calf a closed seam. Hesk's satchel: the log (the entries of the 118th, 119th, 120th, the fork, the crest, and the inventory of the 129th), the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and note, Ephram's and Vell's letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark, and Oryn's letter on bark. **He has Hesk's brass straightedge, and it reads true in his hand.** He has said "Noted" to Teague three times. He has not answered Ephram.
   852	
   853	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst in this movement. **Left arm slung high, all eight marks taken (read by Oryn at the fork on the 122nd), still under the order: it holds nothing until Oryn says, and she has not said; five weeks on the 131st.** It took no part in her two shots in the Hall, and it held nothing in a fall on the ice on the 127th. **Right ankle taped, never mended**; she walks on the whole sole; Oryn left her a roll at the fork. **She carries Oryn's crossbow on its strap, spanned, with one bolt in it**; she cannot span it; she shot it twice in the Hall, one-handed, and hit twice. She has not asked for the ankle again. She has not crossed, touched or tried the fourth line.
   854	
   855	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **Right shoulder: bolted on the 114th and mended; opened on the Hall's wall on the 119th and mended a second time ("It will not hold three times this season"); bound on the 120th by Teague's healer and Oryn in four yards of webbing, with a drover's turn; the knot not to be undone except by that woman, who looked at it on the 129th and said "It'll do."** He carries the kit on his left side. He lays six bowls and, since the letter, has left the sixth out. He took the fence: "Ours,".
   856	
   857	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Points set on a fire and a lamp only, always outside a line. **Fifty-two sheets** (eleven, twenty-nine, twelve), in three heaps in the back room. **Forty-one kinds** at the two sites done properly; thirty-three of them, and none strange, on the Hall's one wall; the lone line seen there and not measured. Three perimeters of one size and the fourth site's radius by the bend, from outside: four hundred and forty yards and two strides. **The kit's field ledger, given her by Seln.** The leaf of the three places, in four copies (one to a box, one in her coat with the trials leaf). **The carter's map with four rings, the third moved and the fourth unlabelled, and a pencil line through the three paced centres and on off the hide.** The Ternhall brass. She has said "Someone made this pattern." once, aloud, and nobody answered. **Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid.** She paid six marks without counting and keeps the purse.
   858	
   859	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Unmarked. He shot twice in the Hall and hit twice. He wrote in Karis's book with his own hand. He gave away the ledger. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.** He has placed "the sheet with two" (the Hall and the fourth site). He has not named the index, the stroke and the bar, or a date for the cordon. Oryn does not know that he is the source of Shadow.
   860	
   861	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. At Thornwater, "on the route". **She has been paid what she set**: three readings, and all of it told, with his hand on her wrist; she has said "It's paid." **She has not forgiven him, and the answer that she kept is still kept.** Her finding stands: she has never felt such a thing in anyone, and does not know what right would look like in him. She has no theory and has been offered none. She knows the mechanism, and the nine by Path. She has the mill's grey jenny, her pannier and rope. Her own brown mule is with the crew. She was three weeks late at the ford with the oxen on the 125th. The old woman's knee, mended at Millrace on the 113th, has held. Her letter: *Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
   862	
   863	**Teague.** Unread and unasked. **Four marks poorer for the weir slip, which he forfeited.** He knows: six went into the Fallow and came out; one walked out of the Stair declaring more than one thing; six came out of the drowned hall wet; a strip of brass runs through three places on a hide and off its edge. He has asked "What are you." once and been answered "I don't have one." He knows nothing of the mechanism or of anything working inside a line. His term: "After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." He has given the five the forty-mark fence and has the cold road. His crew is unnamed.
   864	
   865	**Pike's book and the wall.** **Nine lines** under the five names; the ninth is the Stair, with *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The season line said and written. **No number against it.** **Eleventh on the cloth, unchanged.** He has turned the ledger toward Cael once. The Fallow hundred and the Stair's three hundred lie in his box unmarked.
   866	
   867	**Objects and money.** **Ninety-six marks in hand** (a hundred and two, less six to the inn). The reserve sealed in the third box, unopened. Three boxes against the warm wall of the back room. **The kit on the table**: the case in tarred canvas, the cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, the mallet; the ledger beside it. Two brass straightedges. The brown mule (the text does not say where she is stabled). Oryn's crossbow, hook and one bolt; four bolts are in the wold-wyrm. Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman, "not this side of the thaw"; a reply to Ephram. The fence at forty is taken and not yet begun.
   868	
   869	**The Quieting, as known.** Four sites on one edge of brass. The Fallow Ring (stake 31), the Long Stair (32, on two bars, one with the stroke and the bar; the same mark on the kit's unused stake) and the Drowned Hall (33, old, no second) entered. The fourth seen and paced from outside on the 123rd: twice the radius by the bend of its cordon; stakes numbered 34 with the stroke and the bar, a pace apart; old ruts kept a hundred and ninety paces off; a standing grey shape that nobody could make out. **Not entered, not named, not dated, and not tried: no Arbiter has been taken to that line and what it does is unknown.** At the other three the live Arbiters stop at the line and Cael's fragments answer inside; whether a line twice the size does anything more is Lira's question and is unanswered.
   870	
   871	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. The still place: the six. That fragments answer inside a line: the six. The Compact's side: a completion report on the Fallow; what the rim saw at the Stair; and now a clerk's packet saying that a crew kept a kit. Nobody outside the six knows that anything works inside. Nobody has said why. Nobody has put a verb between a made circle, an age with no ruler, four on a line, and a boy.
   872	
   873	**Words said aloud.** *The Quieting* (Karis's, for the box; once more at the Hall's stone). [UNBOUND] once, in Movement 6, and not since. "Compact" aloud once, by Seln, in Movement 5; in this movement only in Cael's log and his letter to Vell. "The still place" aloud to Oryn and, by its description, to the four. "Someone made this pattern." once. "wold-wyrm" and "the drowned hall" as a carter's words, by Oryn.
   874	
   875	**Open threads.** A line that runs off the edge of a calfskin. A fourth circle behind a fence of iron, and a thing in it that the dusk kept. Whether twice the size is twice anything else. An arm to be released by a healer two days off, and an ankle that nobody has mended. A shoulder in webbing that will not bear a third mending. A captain's term. A fence at forty. A number that a book-keeper would not write. A dead man's ledger with an index and a mark that nobody will read. A locked case. A place under a breastbone, left alone. A rope. A letter to a friend on a bluff. And a question that was asked once at a table, which nobody answered.
