    60	
    61	It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
    62	
    63	There was a broad margin down the leaf's right side, ruled, and headed *Observations of the office.*
    64	
    65	He had filled that margin on some hundreds of summaries. It was a thing he was known for, and juniors were shown his annotations in their second year, as examples of how much could be said in a space two fingers wide.
    66	
    67	He read the leaf from its head to its seal.
    68	
    69	*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
    70	
    71	There were four lines, and he could find nothing wrong with any of them.
    72	
    73	He laid his left hand on the blotter. It did not like mornings. Below the window the porter was feeding the pigeons from his paper bag, and they had come to him in their half-circle.
    74	
    75	*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
    76	
    77	*Pending jurisdiction.* So they had gone where no district ran.
    78	
    79	He was glad of it. It was not a thing to write in a margin, and he noted it only in passing, as he would have noted a reading that came out a hair above tolerance.
    80	
    81	He took up his pen.
    82	
    83	There were true things he might have set there. He considered each of them, as he considered everything, and with each he saw at once what would be done with it. He had written three true sentences about this practitioner before, in an advisory, as exactly as he could make them, and somebody had lifted them out and used them as a warrant. He had petitioned to be heard and been refused in two lines. He had written a finding by a candle and addressed it to nobody; it was in the lowest drawer, under a tab, and no proceeding had ever come that was worth sending it to. The one sentence of his that they still needed he had withheld, and a clerk had entered its absence on a form.
    84	
    85	He had forty years' practice in saying what was so, and he had learned in this one year that a true sentence, once it left his desk, belonged to whoever picked it up.
   285	He folded each in three, and wrote a name on the outside, and a town, and he put no house on either, for there was nowhere to answer to. Karis had said it at a bare table: there wasn't anybody else whose address would still be an address. He had become one of the people it was true of.
   286	
