   308	
   309	It lay in the pocket inside the file's front cover, where slips of advice were put. The clerk had not put it there. It carried no office's mark, and no initial, and it was in a plain copying hand that might have been anybody's.
   310	
   311	The clerk had met such slips before, though not often. There was one of them on this file already. It was older than any other paper in it, older than the first leaf, and it had given the subject a designation that two careful officers had since tried to trace and could not. That one sat at the head of the file, and this one had come to its foot.
   312	
   313	Such a slip did not explain itself. It said what was to be entered, and a clerk did not ask it for its authority, any more than one asks a pen. The slip advised, and the entry was made as advised.
   314	
   315	The clerk dipped the pen and wrote a fifth line under the four, in the same ink.
   316	
