   334	
   335	"That is what they will be asking one another."
   336	
   337	"They can't even draw it." She jerked her head at the cart, where a printed sheet lay rolled in the second box. "They've a key to the symbols where we're sitting."
   338	
   339	Cael had the book shut on his knee.
   340	
   341	He had written it all down. It was on the leaf, in order, with one line in it that had no name. He had been keeping that kind of list since he was fourteen, and he had always kept it where nobody could read it over his shoulder. He looked round the fire, at four faces lit from below, and counted them, from habit.
   342	
   343	He said it aloud. He had never once done that with an inventory.
   344	
   345	"I have nine things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no enrollment, no protection — and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them, and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me, and one taken in earnest from a friend, and the machine's own maintenance records in a case under that canvas, which we can't file and can't publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can't be spent."

