editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:1:# Chapter 6 — West, and Inside
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:149:"That's not my knot. A woman tied that who's kept shoulders shut for forty cold terms, and I can't read through her web without undoing it." She put two fingers under the lowest turn, and took them away. "She says when. I'll look at it beside her, when she's by, and you'll have what the pair of us make of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:295:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:141:"No," said Cael. "You're the slowest of us on a slope, with that arm, and you know it. And somebody has to keep the strip going ahead of her, which is Seln. It's mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:71:It had gone blue on the hundred and fifty-third night, as the ferryman had said that it would, with a groan that woke half the inn. He had walked it the next morning from bank to bank with an iron bar, sounding at every tenth stride, and had come home and cut spruce boughs. By noon there was a double row of them standing in the ice, a cart's width apart, going away up the north water as far as a person could see: his track. It swung wide at the bend above the landing, a furlong out of the straight, to keep clear of the place where the side water came in under the western bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:By the next morning there was a square trodden in the snow under the great ash, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner. Somebody had carried a bench down from the upper inn and set it along the western edge. Lira took one end of it. Brom took the middle, since a shoulder in webbing was of no use in a square. At the other end, with her staff across her knees and a splinting board at her feet, sat a lean old woman in a cap, who looked at the knot on Brom's webbing as she sat down and at nothing else that belonged to the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:Seln had stood at the north-west peg throughout, with his hands in his sleeves. He had a peeled hazel wand under one arm, and before the pairs were made he had cut a notch in it with his thumbnail, as he had on every morning since a clerk's bag went over the river on the hundred and thirtieth day. There were eighteen notches now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"May I read that?" he said. "The ankle. It's the one thing I've got that does nothing unless there's a hand on somebody. I can't shut it for you. I could say how it lies."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:He did not deny it. A Force paid where the line came out of him. Every time that he had put his Path through a thing, the thing had gone where he sent it, and something of the same size had come home along the bone; and the bone kept the account. He had known that at twenty, and had not then known what the sum would look like with a good many cold terms of it added up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:She had built a good deal on that. She had gone into rooms where she was the smallest person and the lowest tier, and had argued from it. She had copied marks off stone for years with nobody's leave, and nobody's belief either. A rubbing was a fact about a wall, and it did not matter in the least whose hand had held the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:47:Over the man's head the ferry's cable hung bare from bank to bank, with rime on it. She had made her last crossing. No post would come over until the thaw, so that a letter to Denvash and another to a yard in a western city would lie unanswered for as long as the ice chose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:In the west each of them had been a line in a registry: an Ember of the third rank in Iron, and a Wind of the first; an Iron Skin who had stopped at Copper and said so without shame; and a Shadow, Bronze, whom fifteen years of service had taught to be overlooked. Nobody out here had asked any of them for a tier since the ninth day. The board at the foot of the meadow priced the job and never the person. By its reckoning the five of them stood eleventh among the crews on a sheet of sailcloth, which was all the rank that this country kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:191:He held it where it was. It was a defector's craft, and in any hands but its owner's it would tell a trained eye exactly who had stood near enough to teach it; and the man who had stood near enough was ten feet off, with his back turned, keeping a door. Cael had opened it twice out here, where nobody could see who might carry the seeing west. He did not mean to open it a third time for the sake of a page in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:Pike wrote it, and said it over to the meadow, as he said everything. Forty fires had burned along that water in the dry months, with a cart or a tent to each. There were three now, and a great many black rings in the snow where the others had been; the hide-woman sat at the nearest with a skin across her knees, and she was the only person in half a mile of grass who lifted her head to hear what a crew had paid. Then his pen went down the leaf to the last line under their names, which was the season's, and stopped beside the ruled space at the end of it.

