editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:On the hundred and fifty-eighth morning there were twenty-eight notches in Seln's hazel wand, and a boy of sixteen was standing in front of the bench with his cap in his two hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:He had come down from the upper inn ahead of his captain. He had gone red before he was within ten yards of the pegs, and by the time he reached the bench's end he was red to the collar. Cael, who was binding a rag round the grip of his stave, watched him find his first word and lose it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:47:Cael went back to four strides, with his heart going. The thing in front of him had changed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:81:He had the whole river from that roof. A cart had left the green. It was a heavy thing on four wheels, behind two mules, and its driver had cut the bend to save himself the furlong. It stood eighty yards out, a little upstream of the landing, with its tail gone down through the ice to the bed's edge and its pole pointing at the sky. The two mules were on their knees in the traces. Something dark was moving in the broken water behind the tailboard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:87:"One at a time. Ten yards between you. If she talks, you lie down, and you stay down till she's done talking. And you'll take my line. Not that. The long one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:103:It was the only fragment of the ten that was of the smallest use to him there, and he knew it before he had gone twenty yards. Under the green boughs the ice was a floor. It was two hands thick and as blue as a slate, and when he set his weight on it the weight went out from under his boots in a wide shallow dish, evenly, on every side, and was lost in the mass of the sheet. Sound ice felt so, and he walked on it a good deal faster than he would have dared by eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:115:He went on so, a pace and a half at a time, feeling for the line where the thick ice ended, and marking it. Brom came after him at ten yards. Seln came ten yards behind Brom, and stepped where the big man had stepped. It took nine shingles to reach the cart, and by the seventh the one in his own hand had begun to swim before his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:157:Seln had gone to their heads by a way of his own, well out to the left, and Cael had not once had to tell him where to tread. He had his coat off and laid across the near mule's eyes. He had the end of the ferryman's line through the ring of the cart's pole, with a knot in it that would never be picked, and he looked back along eighty yards of rope to the round shed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:171:Cael did not see the drum turn. He saw the rope come up out of the snow. It rose along its whole length, dripping, and drew as straight as a ruled line from the shed to the cart's pole, and creaked. The two cart mules felt the collar ease. They got their feet under them, with Seln's voice going on between their ears as evenly as a clock, and the tilted bed began to rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He lay on his face on two hands of blue ice and breathed. His left leg was wet to the knee, where a plate had let him through as he went down, and the cold of it had begun to bite; and his head felt like a room from which the lamp has been carried out. He heard the cart mules blowing. He heard a ferryman's rope go slack and fall across eighty yards of snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:261:On the far side of the lamp Karis had moved her three heaps to the table's end. She had the kit's case open before her, with the cord out of it, and beside the cord a peg of peeled hazel, cut to the length of the iron stake and pointed with a knife. Under her two hands, open for the first time since a quiet man had put it into them on a road, was the dead surveyor's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:265:She drew the cord toward her as she spoke. It was a plain thing, of tarred hemp no thicker than a pencil, with a knot tied in it at every fifth stride and a brass eye whipped into one end, where a stake would go through to hold it to the ground. A man had measured a circle and the floor inside it with nothing more than that, and a mallet, and his own two feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:"Then it will wait for your eyes." She shut the book, and ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "On the hundred and sixty-eighth I shall want all five of you in the inn yard at noon, with dry hands. I have had this cord for forty-six days and I have not yet seen anybody measure with it. It is time that we learned how he did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:7:There was a brown river now, fifty yards wide and going like a mill-race, with the white stones under it and the bar under it as well. Nothing showed of thirty yards of gravel but a long seam in the current, where the water humped and ran on. It had brought down a whole cold term of snow from the hills, and it made a noise that he could feel in the shingle through his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:31:Brom had the box-rope uncoiled. He did not ask her leave; he passed it round her waist, and knotted it, and took three turns of the other end about his own left forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:39:They went over in her line: Brom at the rope's length, with the mule's cheek-strap in the same fist, and Karis on the upstream side with both hands shut on the pack-girth, and Cael, and Seln last. The river leaned on them the whole way, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it after the first breath. He kept his left boot in a groove that he could not see, and his eyes on the back of Lira's neck, and the read shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:43:He saw it go: the right ankle, the one that nobody had ever mended, rolling outward on the green. Her right arm flew wide. Under the grey wool her left started to come, and stopped at a knot, and the rope came taut in Brom's fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:77:She tied off the linen, and sent the old man away with a word about keeping it dry, and only then got up and turned to them. Her eyes passed over Cael as they would have passed over the gatepost. They went to four yards of webbing, and to a knot upon it that she had not tied; they went down to a boot with river water running out of its lacings; and they stopped at a fold of grey wool, and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:Lira's right hand went up to her collar-bone, where the knot was, and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:101:Lira looked at the healer for the length of a breath. Then she bent her head, and got the knot between her teeth, and worked at it with her right hand until it gave. The grey wool slid down. She drew it off over her head; and the arm that had lain against her breast since a white floor on the Fallow, through a stair and a hall and the whole of a cold term, came down by its own weight and hung at her side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:She got up, and walked round him at her stride and a half, and studied four yards of webbing from the top turn to the bottom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:149:"That's not my knot. A woman tied that who's kept shoulders shut for forty cold terms, and I can't read through her web without undoing it." She put two fingers under the lowest turn, and took them away. "She says when. I'll look at it beside her, when she's by, and you'll have what the pair of us make of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:"Four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from the iron to its middle," said Karis. "I had that by the bend, from outside. I have not one sheet of the place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:199:He gave it to her in order. There was a surveyor's cord, knotted at the fives. Four people had measured the inn yard with it, and had forty-two strides; he had measured it three times and had a piece under forty-two each time. There were two strips of brass, which were lying under her eyes. In his hand, against marks pricked in a table, one of them had come up a hair short and the other had gone a hair long, and in every other hand they were true. He had not touched any of the three since, and he had ruled *hold*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:289:Every count that he kept had stopped at that water, and they were all about to move again. There was a second hazel wand under Seln's arm with forty notches in it. There were eleven lines in a one-armed man's book. There was a girl up at the inn who had lifted a bench with two hands that afternoon, for no reason, and put it down, and lifted it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:63:The kit's case lay open on the deal. It had come up out of a hole in the ground sixteen days ago, on Brom's good shoulder, and nobody had yet done more than look into it. There was the cord, coiled, with a knot at every fifth stride. There was the mallet. And there was the stake, which Karis had lifted out and was holding in both hands, as a person holds something that belongs to somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:69:"You could knock it into the yard and have done," said Lira. "You've wanted a peg for that cord since the marsh."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:83:"The ferryman's shed, after dark. It has hurdles round it, and it's a hundred yards from the nearest window." He held up a finger for each. "Three rules. Nothing where anybody could watch who might carry what they saw over that river at the thaw. Shadow stays shut; I'll not spend what protects the person it came from. And nothing with a hand on anybody unless I've asked first and been told yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:"Three." Lira was looking at the grey wool across her own breast, and at a knot in it that was not hers. "She said first snow, near enough. Then she said that she'd say when, and then she wrote us a letter with a thaw in it. So mine runs out on the same day as his, and I can't so much as shovel while I wait for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:137:"With none. It's the coming down that I've been told about." She settled the sling against her collar-bone, where a healer had knotted it. "So I'll sit here and count for you, and you'll do it badly, and I'll enjoy that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:The horse-walk was a ring of beaten earth round the drum, eight strides across. He chose the post that faced him on the far side. Wind had been in his feet since a painted floor, a long while ago; it was the first of the ten, and hers, and for a hundred and thirty days it had leaned him toward the first stride of everything while he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:157:There was a crack like a wet sheet shaken out. The hurdles bellied, and straw went up off the floor in a ring, and he was at the far post with his hand almost upon it. He had not crossed the space between. He had left one place and come to another, and the eight strides had been taken out of the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:169:The third time he asked for half the distance. He came down four strides out, in the open, beside the drum, and the count was exactly as long as it had been for eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:267:Teague's five went up toward the cold road in file, a crew's length apart, and not one of them looked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:293:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:"A cord is the oldest ruler that there is, and very nearly the worst," she said. "It stretches when it is wet and it draws up when it dries, and if you let it hang in a belly it will read long. So you will hold it as he must have held it, and you will all hold it alike."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:She showed them how it was done. She was a long time about it, since she had never before had anybody to show. The brass eye went over the peg. The cord was run out to its last knot, which was the fourth, and drawn until it had left the snow along the whole of its length and no tighter. A pebble was set down at the end. Then the peg was lifted, and carried to the pebble, and driven there, and the cord was run out again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:"He has knotted it at the fives, which is five strides of his own. Four knots is a length. So a person counts lengths, and then knots, and then whatever strides are left over, by this." She held up a slip of ash with a notch at each end, a stride long by the dead man's reckoning. "It is not clever. It only wants doing the same way every time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:13:She did it herself, and the yard came to one length and four knots and two strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:19:Lira could not drive a peg, and Seln drove it for her. She ran the cord out with her right hand, and drew it, and set her pebbles with the toe of the great boot. She arrived at the scratch on the trough with two strides in hand. Brom did it left-handed, and came to forty-two. Seln did it without appearing to hurry and without any waste, as he did everything, and had the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:"Good," said Karis. She was pleased, and it made her brisk. "It is a sound cord, and a level yard, and four people can agree about it. Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:25:He had watched four of them do it, and he did it as they had: he ran the cord out, and drew it until it left the snow, and set his pebble, and moved the peg, and ran it out again, and counted four knots along from there. Then he took the slip of ash and laid it down from the fourth knot toward the trough, once, and went to lay it a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:He did it again, with her walking at his elbow and her eyes on his fingers. He drew the cord exactly clear of the snow and not a hair beyond. She bent at the pebble, and at the peg, and at each knot as he came to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:47:She set them. She drove the peg herself. He did no more than run the cord out and hold its end; and when the slip of ash had been laid down once from the fourth knot, there was room for only half of it again before the line cut in the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:49:She took the cord out of his hand. Without moving the peg she drew the last length herself, and laid the slip, and laid it again. The second time its notch came down upon the scratch as a key comes into a lock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:She had them in the store behind the chimney before the cord was coiled. She cleared the deal with her forearm. The older strip of brass came out of its cloth, and she laid it down on the bare wood, and pressed a needle into the table at each of its two ends, so that there were two bright pricks in the deal exactly as far apart as the strip was long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:Cael gave it to her. She pricked its length beside the other. It was a hand longer, as it had always been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:113:*Claim: something. Evidence: a cord, two edges, a hair. Ruling: hold.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:117:On the night of the hundred and seventieth Karis laid the dead man's cord out along the floor of the store, from the door to the meal-sacks and back, and measured it with a ruler.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:119:She did it on her knees, by her own lamp, with a point set on the wick. She had the older strip of brass, and a pencil, and the patience that she had been praised for as a child and blamed for ever since. The brass went down beside the cord at the brass eye. She made a dot on the boards at its far end, and slid it along to the dot, and made another. It was how she had once carried a straight line across a calfskin, and it was as good as its owner's care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:121:There were thirty-one lays and a piece from the eye to the first knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:She had done this once before, on the hundred and sixteenth evening, at a fire, on the day after that cord came up out of the ground. She had the figure in her trials leaf. She did not look at it until she had her new one written down, since a person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:125:Then she took the leaf out of her coat. *Thirty-one, and a piece under half.* The cord had not altered by the width of her pencil in fifty-four days. She did the second knot, and the third, and the fourth, and they were as the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:127:So the cord was true. She had known that it would be; she had watched four people get one figure out of it in a yard. She had measured it regardless, since *I had known that it would be* was not a sentence that she allowed in a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:137:Nobody had been able to tell her, and so she had worked it out for herself on the walk home, and had found that it steadied her wonderfully. The card was not a fact about Karis; it was a fact about a plate, and a table of figures, and whoever had last adjusted them. A ruler said what a ruler said. If it said different things of the same length, then somebody had a bad ruler, and the length was exactly what it had been before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:141:And now there was a cord that read forty-two in four hands and forty-one and a piece in a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:143:She made herself look at it squarely, as she would have looked at another person's error. The cord was sound, and the yard had not moved. The peg had been hers, and the pebbles, which she had set with her own fingers, the third time, so that he had done nothing at all but hold an end. If her sentence was true, then what she had seen could not have happened. She had seen it three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:147:She could feel every part of it but one. It would begin with a boy in whom no instrument had ever found a tier. It would have three floors in it, where five people's Arbiters stopped at a line and something of his did not. It would have a cord, and a hair. And somewhere in the middle of all those nouns it would need a verb, to say what the one had to do with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:On the trials leaf, under the figure for the cord, she entered what she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:*Hundred and sixty-eighth: one cord, two edges. Four hands agree. One does not, by a piece and by a hair. Cord re-measured on the hundred and seventieth: sound. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:163:She had meant to go to her blanket. She had got as far as putting the cord away, coiled, in its place in the tarred case, beside a stake that had never been in ground; and then her hand had gone to the limp little ledger instead of to the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:209:Anchor he tried in the shed, on the capstan bar. He set a point with his palm, and held it, and the other nine went thin and far off, as they had in a drowned hall. Brom leaned his good shoulder on the bar and could not stir it. At thirty Cael let go. He had been gripping the drum with his other hand, to stay upright, and had not known it. Afterward he set a second, and took it down with his hand on it, as he had once taken down another person's at a knot. He paced off from a third to learn where it left his sight. It was sure at ten feet, and faint at twelve, and at fourteen there was nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:215:*That is the ten. Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:241:Seln cut the hundredth notch on the landing, on the two hundred and thirtieth morning, and there was hardly room left on the wand to take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:253:"Sixty yards," he said, when Brom went by. "Four-strand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:257:"I'd not have you forget what it is. The first boat brings cordage. She'll bring it for whoever's on my landing with the money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:265:Cael had not touched the cord again. Karis had not offered it, and he had not asked. It lay coiled in its tarred case beside a stake that had never been driven, and one pale hair lay pressed in the fold of a notebook, and for ninety-five days the two of them had kept his ruling as carefully as if either had known what it was a ruling about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:289:Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:23:"What it buys me. You came out of three of them." Teague's eyes went to a sling, and to four yards of webbing, and came back. "I'd not say that you came out cheap."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:By the next morning there was a square trodden in the snow under the great ash, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner. Somebody had carried a bench down from the upper inn and set it along the western edge. Lira took one end of it. Brom took the middle, since a shoulder in webbing was of no use in a square. At the other end, with her staff across her knees and a splinting board at her feet, sat a lean old woman in a cap, who looked at the knot on Brom's webbing as she sat down and at nothing else that belonged to the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:There was a bundle of sticks in the snow by the bench, cut to every length, and Cael chose a stave of ash as long as his arm, since the only steel that he owned was a knife. The tall one took a pole. It was seven feet of the same wood, as thick as a wrist, with a wad of rag bound over one end; and its owner carried it out to the middle of the square as that spear had been carried on every road that Cael had seen it on, upright, and grounded at each stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:He stood four strides off and waited, since waiting had served him in every yard that he had ever entered. He would see the first thing come, and read it, and know the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:57:It had not been swung. It had come straight, with the whole length of two arms and a long step behind it, and had covered four strides in the time that he took to see it start. He had gone back, and the rag had simply followed him and arrived.
