editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:89:"I do not fight in yards," he said, when Teague's look came to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:153:At the bench's far end a lean old woman got up, and looked at a knot, and went away up the bank without a word to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:305:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:103:It was as thin as a rail. It was white, and there were eight small marks along it in a row, as pale now as the skin about them. She lifted it, and it rose slowly, and shook, and the fingers opened and shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:199:He gave it to her in order. There was a surveyor's cord, knotted at the fives. Four people had measured the inn yard with it, and had forty-two strides; he had measured it three times and had a piece under forty-two each time. There were two strips of brass, which were lying under her eyes. In his hand, against marks pricked in a table, one of them had come up a hair short and the other had gone a hair long, and in every other hand they were true. He had not touched any of the three since, and he had ruled *hold*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:"A hundred and sixteen. Five on. Three off," said Brom. "A hundred and eighteen in hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:239:"These are cut with marks. I have fifty-two sheets of them. They are the same forty-one kinds on three floors, and they stand in the same order on all three. People do not copy a tale so, or a list of the dead. Between the courses there is a line that carries the measure of the circle it stands in." She drew breath. "I think that they are instructions. A thing that has its own size written on its wall is telling somebody how it is made. I cannot read one word, and I know what kind of writing it is, as any of you would know a bill from a letter across a room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:He paid at the gate, under the axe-head. He counted forty marks onto the top bar, and put a thumb-mark shaped like a wedge at the foot of Pike's slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:271:"A hundred and sixteen in hand," said Brom, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:197:It was not a thing that a captain liked, to send one of his own to another crew's bench to be taught, and Teague priced it as he priced any stage. It would cost him nothing in marks. What it would cost him was the boy's seeing, every morning for a cold term, that there was a thing which his own captain could not show him. He had known captains who would not pay that; their people stood in the lower half of Pike's cloth, or had left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:289:"That's a copper to the smith each time," said Brom. "I'll have Karis enter it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:19:Lira could not drive a peg, and Seln drove it for her. She ran the cord out with her right hand, and drew it, and set her pebbles with the toe of the great boot. She arrived at the scratch on the trough with two strides in hand. Brom did it left-handed, and came to forty-two. Seln did it without appearing to hurry and without any waste, as he did everything, and had the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:63:"Those four marks will not move. The table is not going anywhere." She put the older strip into Lira's right hand. "Lay it to its two. Tell me what you see at the far end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:65:Lira laid it. The end of the brass sat on the prick as a boot sits in its own print. It did the same for Brom, and for Seln. Hesk's edge, tried by the three of them in turn, lay upon its own two marks so truly that Karis had to bring the lamp down to find them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:87:He laid Hesk's edge to its own marks. It was the straightedge that an old man had sent over a river to a boy, with a note round it that said *true to a hair*. Cael watched a small pale woman bend to its far end, and stay there, and slowly move her one hair until it lay along the wood on the wrong side of the prick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:She had built a good deal on that. She had gone into rooms where she was the smallest person and the lowest tier, and had argued from it. She had copied marks off stone for years with nobody's leave, and nobody's belief either. A rubbing was a fact about a wall, and it did not matter in the least whose hand had held the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:She had never in her life wanted an object as she wanted that page. It was not only that it would save her a year. She had been right before, in an annex, with three leaves on a trestle before it was light; she had been right, and it had made no difference, since she had had nothing to show a seat but her own rubbings and her own word. Stone proved that marks existed. It took a text to say what they meant, and it took a text that somebody else had kept to make anybody believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:One of the ten had sent its bill to somebody else. On the hundred and eighty-first day he took Lira's long knife up to the smith, with a copper of his own, and brought it back with an edge on it, and laid it beside her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:Cael had Hesk's book inside his coat, and he did not need to take it out, since every figure in it had stopped at this river. There were nine lines under five names in a one-armed man's ledger, and a sheet of sailcloth by the inn's hearth with those names on its eleventh line, and neither would change by a stroke while the holds were shut. There were ninety-six marks in a purse. There were twenty-seven uses written against the ten fragments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:Karis took out the purse. She had paid six to the keeper without looking, four days before, with her eyes on a door. Today she opened her notebook first, and did the sum, and did it again beneath; then she counted twenty marks onto the tarred oak in four heaps of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:243:"Seventy-six in hand," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:281:*Hundred and thirty-third day. Lowmarch, the lower inn. The cold stay is paid: four a month, five months, twenty in front. In hand, seventy-six. The reserve is not counted here. Nine lines, and no number against the season, on purpose. Eleventh on the cloth. The board has a roof, a well-house and a fence. The fence is ours, and we go out to it on the hundred and thirty-eighth. The roof is ours as well, at five, paid when it has kept a thaw out. The mule stands in the ferryman's shed, at a fee he has not asked for and will.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:303:"Every figure that I keep has stopped," he said. "The purse won't move till the fence. The cloth can't move at all. I've nine lines and no post, and a number that I've promised her not to change."

