editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:287:He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:295:*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:297:He read the last of it over. He did not like it, and it was true; he kept the pencil's point on the leaf beneath it for a long breath and made no mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:"A cord is the oldest ruler that there is, and very nearly the worst," she said. "It stretches when it is wet and it draws up when it dries, and if you let it hang in a belly it will read long. So you will hold it as he must have held it, and you will all hold it alike."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:"So it's him," said Brom slowly. "Nothing's ever been able to read him. Not a plate, nor a healer. I don't see why a string should be the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:She had not made it for brass. She had made it as a girl, in a hall with a counter in it, on the day when a registrar laid a card before her that said *Iron*, and a numeral, and *certified*. The girl beside her had wept at hers. Karis had read her own twice, and had asked what instrument it had been taken with, and how that instrument was kept true; and the registrar had looked at her as at a child who inquires what the sky is fastened to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:141:And now there was a cord that read forty-two in four hands and forty-one and a piece in a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:*Hundred and sixty-eighth: one cord, two edges. Four hands agree. One does not, by a piece and by a hair. Cord re-measured on the hundred and seventieth: sound. Cause: not found. No inference entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:She read that over, and left it. It was true, and it was very much less than she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:167:She had looked at them on every night since the ice road, and they had not improved. They were small, and upright, and they pressed, like everything in that hand; but they were not his own. A man does not invent four signs for the place where he is standing. He had copied them out of something, carefully, as a carter copies the mark of a hold onto a bale. They were somebody's name for a square hole with a stair in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:She had never in her life wanted an object as she wanted that page. It was not only that it would save her a year. She had been right before, in an annex, with three leaves on a trestle before it was light; she had been right, and it had made no difference, since she had had nothing to show a seat but her own rubbings and her own word. Stone proved that marks existed. It took a text to say what they meant, and it took a text that somebody else had kept to make anybody believe her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:183:She knew where such texts were kept, and had known for years, having once read the place's name on the spine of a catalogue that she was not allowed to open. It was a long way from this room. It was on the other side of a river, and of a Line, and of a great deal that five people had walked away from; and there was one among the five whom it would cost more than all the rest together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:187:She would not ask until she could say the whole of it at once, and say what it rested on, and say what it did not. She had been giving it to them in pieces because the pieces were all that she had. A person does not ask a friend to walk into the place that he fears most on the strength of a piece.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other. It was like shutting an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:*Storm, from Daeva: one lane. Ten counts late; clean at eleven. The read goes blind to the ground while it lasts. Three in a row is as many as I have laid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:Teague's three had not spoken, and their captain was not even among them. He stood by the bench with his thumbs in his belt. He had looked at a place on the trodden snow, once, before the word was given. When it was given the spear was standing in that place, and the bow was where the spear had been, and the boy had the sack over the pegs and was waiting to be told whether he might put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:237:Cael entered that as he would have entered a touch taken. *He is better at this than I am. The cloth has said so since we first read it, and the cloth is right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:275:"In pieces. I had not the right to say all of it until I had looked in every other place where the answer might lie, and I have looked. It is not in fifty-two sheets. It is not in that case, or in his book." She had a leaf before her with a heading on it and eleven lines. She did not read from it. "Whoever cut those courses did not think in stone. Stone is where a thing is put when it is finished. It was written first; and what is written is kept; and the oldest keeping that I know of is on the far side of that river, inside a wall, under an office of the registry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:289:Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"I said that my crew comes, and it comes. But I'll not walk it up to one of those places as it is. In there a Path's a thing that you had yesterday." He looked at the fire while he told it off, as he might have told off a load. "My spear's a tall body with a pole. My bow's a man with a bad hip. The boy's a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:21:"What does it buy us?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:Nobody at that fire could dispute it, and Cael went over the offer as he went over any slip. Inside a line the other four were exactly what this man had said of his own; they had fought there as such, three times, with a bill and a knife and a borrowed bow, and two of them sat here bound up in consequence. He himself was another matter, which Teague did not know. But he had never once stood up against anybody with the ten fragments shut, and holding a thing down on a road was not the same as going without it in a fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:29:"Three touches. A touch is what the one who took it calls, since I've nobody to judge. Over the pegs is a touch." Teague drew one thumb out of his belt. "And nobody strikes what a healer's had her hands on this season. That's her arm, and his shoulder, and my bow's hip. And your left side. I saw the tape on you at a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:51:He had read this person a dozen times across rooms and roads, and never from so near. There was a long quiet face under a wool cap, with pale eyes in it, and a pair of big chapped hands. The feet were set a little apart in the snow, and nothing else about the tall one told him anything whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:He stood four strides off and waited, since waiting had served him in every yard that he had ever entered. He would see the first thing come, and read it, and know the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:63:The read asked next, civilly, as it always did. A pace and a half would have had that pole from the moment that its butt left the snow: its weight, and which way the weight was going, a full beat before his eyes could tell him. It did not feel like doing anything. Not a person in that meadow would have known. Teague had said *not a sigil*, and Cael had no sigil, and never had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:He knew a clerk's way through a rule when he was the one finding it. He had been on the wrong end of such ways since his Kindling, and he knew exactly what they were worth to the person on the other side. He shut the read, and kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:71:His shoulder sang with it. He set his teeth and did what a boy does who has been hit twice from a distance, which was to go at the thing that was hitting him. He beat the pole aside with his stave, with all his arm, and stepped in behind the blow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:113:"It comes out and it goes home. Did you see? Out's quick. You'll not beat *out*; nobody will, off that one. But it has to be gathered again before it can come a second time, and that's slower, because there's seven feet of it to gather." She slapped her knee twice, a quick one and a slow one. "Out. Home. You go on *home*. Not back, and not round. In. Down the outside of the wood, close enough to rub your sleeve on it, till you're past the front hand. Inside the hands a pole's a broom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"May I read that?" he said. "The ankle. It's the one thing I've got that does nothing unless there's a hand on somebody. I can't shut it for you. I could say how it lies."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:145:"It's hers to read. She tied this arm, and she taught me that tape, and she'll have her hands on the pair of them when she says so. If you tell me how it lies, then I'll know a thing about my own foot that I didn't get from the person who can do something about it." She pulled the boot's top straight. "I'd sooner wait."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"Then you've a spear, and a bow, and a boy with a back like a young ox, and three iron bars. And there's a herd lying up over that ledge, by what the boy saw. Shift it as a carter would." She began to lay the webbing. "I've no Path that knits. I've told you so every year, and every year you've thought I'd find one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:205:The bow laughed, from the settle, where he had his bad leg stretched to the heat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:It was the fourth night that he had seen it: two before he went up the cold road, and two since. He had not gone looking; the house on the rise had one window on that side, and a man who has kept a watch on roads for as long as he had does not sit with his back to a window. The light came on after dark and burned for an hour or two, and for the whole of that time a small still shape stood between two hurdles, facing outward. It was the quiet one. Teague had needed two looks to find him, once, in a room. He found him now in one, and took no credit for it, since the man was not trying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:The cold road paid. He had salt work from now to the thaw, and more of it if the pass stayed shut. The bearing paid nothing; the bow had said so twice, and had been right both times. But Teague had told fifty people at a hearth that his crew would come, and he had not said it for a price, and he would have held to it if he had said it to a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:257:He lay in the straw until he had his wind. He had the limit now, and it was a plain one: the load went through him into whatever he stood on, and the ground had to be worth sending it to. The bill had begun already. His right forearm ached to the elbow, dully, like a tooth, and he knew that by morning it would not care to lift a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:303:*The yard: three touches to none. The read asked, and nobody would have seen. No Paths means the ten, whatever I call them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:She was a small brown mule, and she was not his. She belonged to that healer, who was two days off up a shut road, and who had lent her to five people at a hold's gate a long while ago without saying for how long. He led her by the cheek-strap across the landing's planks to the round shed where the ferry's gelding had walked his circle through all the dry months. The gelding was in the smith's stable now, under a blanket. The shed stood empty, with reed hurdles tied between its posts against the wind, and a great wooden drum in the middle of it that had nothing to wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:25:He opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:79:"I want to say what I'm doing with the cold term, since it'll want some of you. I wrote the inventory two nights ago. All ten fragments are in it, and nearly every line says what the thing costs me to keep shut." He had Hesk's book on his knee, and he did not open it. "That's half an inventory. I've carried some of them a long while and I couldn't tell you how far they go, or how they fail. I've a river that nothing can cross and five months. I mean to try them, one at a time, and write down what each does and what it bills."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:125:"What they say. I have fifty-two sheets, and forty-one kinds of mark, and I can tell you the order in which those kinds stand on three floors. I cannot tell you what a single one of them means." She squinted along a strip from the Fallow. "I have never learned to read a thing from the thing alone. Nobody has. There must be something to lay beside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:189:It was full dark outside the hurdles by then, and the quietest of the ten fragments had come up with the dark, as it did at every dusk. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as far as his knees. It was the only fragment that charged him for being shut, a little, from the minute he woke, like a lamp left burning in a room that nobody used. It could have made less of him. He could have stood in that lantern's light and not been there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:Cael read them from the left-hand post. Each was on bark, in the square black hand, with a little picture beside the words for people who had no letters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:251:"I've not looked for one. That's blank on purpose." He tapped the space with the feather. "A blank that a man leaves because he'd nothing to put is a hole in his book. One that he leaves because he'll not put a wrong thing is a kind of entry. I read it out as a blank at first snow, twice, in a full room. Nobody scratched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:295:He read the leaf over. It was a poor thing beside the inventory that he had made four nights ago, which had ten names in it, and a still place, and two promises. This had three fragments and the small plain facts of two evenings in a shed, and under them he had ruled seven lines and left them empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The second time he did not wait to be sure. The rag came straight for his breastbone, and he let it have the place where he had been, by a hand's breadth, so that seven feet of ash went by his left shoulder close enough to stir his hair. *Out.* He felt it stop at the end of two long arms. *Home.* It began to gather; and he went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve rubbing along it, one stride and a second, until the leading hand was behind his elbow and the whole pole was a thing that lay beside him and pointed at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:59:He said it as he would have read a line off a slip, and he was already looking at his bow. But the tall one had not yet gone back to the eastern pegs. That long face was turned toward the bench, where a girl with her arm in a sling had her right hand flat on her knee; and it was in no hurry to turn away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:101:Cael went out onto the shelf, and opened the read, and gave it the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:105:But the read was a pace and a half across. It was a lantern in a fog. It could tell him everything about the yard of river that he stood on and nothing about the next, so that to know a place he had to go to it. When he stepped off the green, level with the cart, he went as a man goes down a cellar stair in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:131:A held point took everything else that he owned. He would be on his knees on a breaking river with the read gone, at the one moment when two men behind him were trusting their lives to what the read said. It was the registry's own craft, moreover, a piece of what the system used to hold people; he would be laying it down on a carters' road in front of a ferryman, and a stranger, and whoever had come out of the inn. And he had held it for a count of sixty, once, and did not know that he could hold it for sixty-one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:Brom got down on the ice like a tree being felled carefully. He had been told on a hillside what a man was worth who could not lift his right arm, and he had set forty-one posts since to find out whether it was so. He went forward now on his left elbow and his knees, with the hedging bill pushed before him, and the read told Cael what a big man lying flat did to a sheet: it spread him. The dish under him was three times as wide as it had been, and a third as deep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:177:It broke back a yard, and the wheels dropped into the gap, and climbed again. It would be so the whole way, he saw: the ice would give under that weight plate by plate until the wheels found something thick enough to bear them. The question was only which way the thick lay. He had the read under his chest and his forearms now, where it gave him the same pace and a half that it gave his boots, and he crawled backward before the mules' knees, feeling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:199:Cael let the read go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:223:"You will." He considered the mule. "She's wound a cart out of my river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:241:*The read: the ground, on ice, from the shelf to the hole and back to the green. In earnest. All my near sight, and the last of it done on my face.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:251:"She's a mule. You tell her, and she does it." Lira shut the hand. "It's a good deal more than I could say of some people on a count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:255:*Iron, from Brom: the read. The mass of a thing and which way it goes. The ground or the air, never both. A pace and a half and not a hand more, so that to know a place I have to go and stand on it. Sound ice takes a weight evenly all round; rotten ice takes it on one side. Frozen ground rings. Leaned on for long, it bills the eyes: an hour after a calf, and more tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:257:*On the ice the other nine were no use. Anchor would have held the lip and cost me the read. Wind would have saved me and nobody else. Compression would have put the load into the thing we were standing on.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:263:"I have left this for thirty-four days," she said. "I did not wish to read another person's findings until I had finished entering my own. I have finished."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:267:She did not read Cael the distances. She laid one finger at the head of the book's first page, above everything else that was written there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:"He begins with this. Four signs in a row, and after them the number, thirty-two. It is how he names the place that he is standing in." Her finger did not move. "They are a clerk's signs. They are not a carver's; I have forty-one kinds of mark from three floors, and these are not cut like any of them. Seln read registry paper for fifteen years, and he does not know the index that they are taken from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:"I cannot read them. I can tell you only that somebody could. A person sat at a desk, and gave that hole in the ground four signs, before this man ever carried a mallet down into it." She turned the leaf. There was one line of pencil on the second page, and a stroke going off toward the edge. "And here is where he stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:It was a road again as soon as the meadow was behind them, and so the hold came down on all five as it had on every road since the Line. Things lived along the roads of that country which hunted whatever a Path gave off, and nobody declared. The sigils stayed in their pockets, and Cael's ten fragments lay shut, and the read asked at every bend what the frozen ruts weighed and was told that it need not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:35:Cael had taken them for boulders. They lay along the eastern end of the row of stones, close in under the rock band, where no snow had drifted: a line of long grey backs, keeled, each as broad as a cart and roofed with plates that overlapped like slates. Snow lay in the seams between the plates. He had last seen such backs on a ledge over a bay, on the seventy-eighth day, a breath before a mountain came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:Karis read out her page in the yard on the hundred and fortieth morning, before it was properly light, with her back against the byre wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:57:"The read," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:59:He had thought of nothing else since the gate. It was the one thing that he owned which did not feel like doing anything; but he had written on a road, on the eighteenth day, that he did not know what looking sounded like out here, and he had never found out. Each of the ten fragments went out of him a little when it answered, as other people's Paths went out of them. The read went out least. Least was not the same as none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:She had Seln pace it along the outside of the standing fence. From the yard gate to the nearest grey back it came to three hundred and fifty yards, which was the distance at which she had once lit a fire under a ledge and seen nothing stir. Brom and the mule dragged the factor's hay sledge up the hill to the last of Teague's posts, and Lira sat on it with the bow across her knees. She had seventeen heads before her there, a hundred and fifty yards off, and the whole of the slope beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:69:He went down to the gate, and opened the read, and gave it the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:He climbed beside Teague's posts and did it again at two hundred yards, and again at sixty, a long way beyond her, where he could see the lichen on the nearest animal's plates. At sixty the read told him nothing whatever about the herd, since its whole world was a pace and a half across. It told him about the slope under his boots. He shut it, and looked back along the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:"The read, three times, for a breath apiece. No answer at three hundred and fifty yards, at two hundred, or at sixty." She read it out as she had read the other. "It is one fragment and one herd and one morning. I shall not write that it is safe. I shall write that three things did not happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:83:"That'll do me," said Cael. "The read, and nothing else. If I want any other fragment on that hill, I'll have to want it and go without."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:87:"They're not asleep," she said. "And they're not eating. I've read feet for longer than I've had a rank, and those aren't the feet of anything that's settled. Look at the snow." She pointed with her chin. "It's off the rock for exactly as far as they lie. That isn't the wind. They've scraped it. They've had every scrap that grows under that lee, and they're lying on an empty plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:103:She had worked that out from a sledge, with one arm tied and a bow that she could not bend, while four people with two good hands apiece stood round a page; he had been reading the ground, and she had been reading the only thing on that hill that could kill them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:133:He looked. A rib of rock stood out of the slope there, a little under the strip, and the wind had blown it clean. It was as bare as anything that they had shovelled, and it was green.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:173:It was the size of an ox, and broader. It had pitched forward onto the trapped shoulder, with its hind feet scrabbling on the frozen turf above, and it rolled a dark eye at him and heaved again. He stopped a pace and a half from its flank and opened the read on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:221:He shut the read. His own fingers, a foot from his face, were a blur; he could see a furlong well enough. Along the hill a small figure got down off a sledge by herself, and then sat in the snow beside it, which she had plainly not meant to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:227:"The read, at a pace and a half, beside a calf in fright. The herd rose and lay down again. There were three other causes present, and I can part none of them from the rest. It is not a test."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:275:"Somebody struck a number into the head of that stake. A person does not make up a number. He takes it from a list." She was frowning at the road ahead. "A list is kept. It is kept under a roof, by people who do not throw lists away."

